    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d280">

                <pb facs="0501" n="455" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg455"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/964½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1919-08-06">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">6 August, 1919</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I would be very much
                    obliged if you would see that the enclosed statement with regard to the Shantung
                        <pb facs="0502" n="456" xml:id="pg456"/> matter<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d280-f1"/> is given to the press and secures as wide publicity as
                    the statement made by Viscount Uchida<anchor n="5" xml:id="d280-f2"/> which, I
                    understand, appears in the American newspapers of this morning, provided, of
                    course, that this form of statement meets with the approbation of your own
                    judgment.</p>
                <p>Cordially and faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Woodrow Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d280-f1" n="4" xml:id="d280-f1-content">
                <p>For text of this statement, see <ref target="frus1919v01#pg_719">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1919, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 719</ref>.</p>
            </note>
